<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Claudio Monteverdi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1567–1643</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Claudio Monteverdi was an Italian composer whose work bridged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> music. After serving the Gonzaga court at Mantua, where he wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/l-orfeo">L'Orfeo</a> (1607), one of the first great operas, he became director of music at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-the-evangelist">St Mark</a>'s Basilica in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1613. In Venice he wrote sacred music, madrigals and late operas, including The Coronation of Poppea, for the new public opera houses.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/claudio-monteverdi.jpg" alt="Claudio Monteverdi" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernardo Strozzi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bernardo_Strozzi_-_Claudio_Monteverdi_(c.1630).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
